>>>>> Delimiter Discrepancies
- Delimiter: A delimiter is a character that separates or marks different parts of a URL.
- Delimiter discrepancy: Cache and origin interpret the same delimiter differently.

Example: /profile;foo.css
  . Origin (Spring): ; is a delimiter → treats it as /profile → returns profile data.
  . Cache: treats the full path as /profile;foo.css → sees .css → caches the response.
Result: Dynamic sensitive data is cached as a static file → Web Cache Deception.

Ruby on Rails uses (. to determine the response format):
 . /profile → HTML → returns profile data.
 . /profile.css → CSS format → no CSS formatter → error.
 . /profile.ico → .ico not recognized → falls back to HTML → returns profile data.
If the cache stores .ico responses, it may cache the profile data as a static file.

Encoded delimiter: /profile%00foo.js
 . Origin (OpenLiteSpeed): %00 is a delimiter → interprets the path as /profile → returns profile data.
 . Cache (Akamai/Fastly): May treat %00foo.js as part of the path → sees .js → may cache the response.
Result: Dynamic profile data can be cached as a static .js resource.

Key idea:
Cache interpretation ≠ Origin interpretation → Dynamic response gets cached → Web Cache Deception

